          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "The peak positions are best indexed by a primitive monoclinic cell. The diagnostic reflections are approximately 002 at 8.34\u00b0, 200 at 13.35\u00b0, 103 at 14.30\u00b0, 004 at 16.72\u00b0, 006 at 25.20\u00b0, and 020/400 at 26.88\u00b0. The systematic absences are consistent with space group No. 14 in the \\(P2_1/n\\) setting:\n\n- \\(0k0:\\;k=2n\\)\n- \\(h0l:\\;h+l=2n\\)\n\nRefined approximate cell:\n\n\\[\na=13.261\\ \\text{\u00c5},\\quad b=6.627\\ \\text{\u00c5},\\quad\nc=21.190\\ \\text{\u00c5},\\quad \\beta=90.95^\\circ,\\quad Z=4.\n\\]\n\nThe lattice and space group are considerably more secure than the atomic coordinates. The CIF below is a chemically regularized direct-space starting model: the AlCl\u2084 tetrahedron and Bi\u2013Se/Br distances are restrained to plausible values, but final coordinates should be obtained by Rietveld refinement against the original instrumental profile.\n\n```cif\ndata_AlBi2Se2BrCl4_primary\n_audit_creation_method\n;\nIndexing of supplied Cu Kalpha1 powder pattern followed by\nchemically restrained direct-space model construction.\nAtomic coordinates are a provisional Rietveld starting model.\n;\n_chemical_name_common                 'AlBi2Se2BrCl4'\n_chemical_formula_sum                 'Al Bi2 Br Cl4 Se2'\n_chemical_formula_weight              824.60\n_cell_formula_units_Z                 4\n\n_cell_length_a                        13.261\n_cell_length_b                         6.627\n_cell_length_c                        21.190\n_cell_angle_alpha                     90\n_cell_angle_beta                      90.95\n_cell_angle_gamma                     90\n_cell_volume                          1861.9\n\n_space_group_crystal_system           monoclinic\n_space_group_IT_number                14\n_space_group_name_H-M_alt             'P 1 21/n 1'\n\n_diffrn_radiation_type                'Cu Kalpha1'\n_diffrn_radiation_wavelength          1.5406\n\nloop_\n_space_group_symop_id\n_space_group_symop_operation_xyz\n1  'x,y,z'\n2  '1/2-x,1/2+y,1/2-z'\n3  '-x,-y,-z'\n4  '1/2+x,1/2-y,1/2+z'\n\nloop_\n_atom_type_symbol\n_atom_type_oxidation_number\nAl   3\nBi   3\nSe  -2\nBr  -1\nCl  -1\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_symmetry_multiplicity\n_atom_site_Wyckoff_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\n_atom_site_U_iso_or_equiv\nBi1  Bi  4  e  0.6800  0.2500  0.0625  1.0  0.015\nBi2  Bi  4  e  0.4800  0.2500  0.1875  1.0  0.015\nSe1  Se  4  e  0.5800  0.6000  0.1000  1.0  0.018\nSe2  Se  4  e  0.5800  0.9000  0.1500  1.0  0.018\nBr1  Br  4  e  0.5800  0.2500  0.3100  1.0  0.020\nAl1  Al  4  e  0.2500  0.2500  0.3800  1.0  0.018\nCl1  Cl  4  e  0.3430  0.4360  0.4380  1.0  0.022\nCl2  Cl  4  e  0.3430  0.0640  0.3220  1.0  0.022\nCl3  Cl  4  e  0.1570  0.4360  0.3220  1.0  0.022\nCl4  Cl  4  e  0.1570  0.0640  0.4380  1.0  0.022\n```\n\nThe \\(P2_1/c\\) description obtained by a conventional change of basis is the same crystallographic solution, not a distinct surviving alternative. The main unresolved issue is therefore coordinate refinement rather than indexing or space-group choice.",
